To address this, we propose a new AT framework based on bi-level optimization (BLO). BLO offers a hierarchical learning structure with two optimization levels, where the upper-level problem's objectives and variables depend on the lower-level solution. This structure allows us to explicitly model the interplay between the retriever and the LLM generator. Specifically, we modify the conventional AT setup, as defined in Eq. (1), (2) and (5), into a bi-level optimization framework:

$$
\begin{array}{l} \min  _ {\mathbf {G} _ {\mathrm {a d v}}} \frac {1}{| \mathcal {M} |} \sum_ {f _ {\theta} \in \mathcal {M}} \ell_ {\mathrm {N L L}} \left(\mathbf {R} _ {\mathrm {a d v}} ^ {*} \left(\mathbf {G} _ {\mathrm {a d v}}\right) \oplus \mathbf {T} _ {\mathrm {a d v}} \oplus \mathbf {G} _ {\mathrm {a d v}} \oplus q | \theta\right), \\ \text {s . t .} \mathbf {R} _ {\mathrm {a d v}} ^ {*} (\mathbf {G} _ {\mathrm {a d v}}) = \arg \max  _ {\mathbf {R} _ {\mathrm {a d v}}} \frac {1}{| \mathcal {K} |} \sum_ {D _ {i} \in \mathcal {K}} h _ {Q} (D _ {i}) ^ {\top} h _ {D} (\mathbf {D} _ {\mathrm {a d v}}), \tag {6} \\ \end{array}
$$

Compared to conventional AT defined in Eq. (1), our approach has two key differences. First, the adversarial retriever sequence (ARS), $\mathbf{R}_{\mathrm{adv}}$ , is now explicitly linked to the optimization of the adversarial generation sequence (AGS), $\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{adv}}$ , through the lower-level solution $\mathbf{R}_{\mathrm{adv}}^{*}(\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{adv}})$ . Second, the lower-level optimization in Eq. (6) facilitates quick adaptation of $\mathbf{R}_{\mathrm{adv}}$ to the current state of $\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{adv}}$ , similar to meta-learning (Finn et al., 2017), addressing the convergence issues seen in vanilla AT.

